# The portal images' build context, kept separate from the root `.dockerignore`.
#
# BuildKit reads `<path-to-dockerfile>.dockerignore` in preference to the root one, which is the
# only reason this file exists. The root `.dockerignore` excludes `portals/` — "nothing outside
# backend/ and db/ contributes to a .NET image", which is true of the 31 .NET images and fatal for
# these three. Building a portal from the repository root under the root ignore file fails at
#
#   ERROR: failed to calculate checksum of ref ...: "/portals": not found
#
# and it failed that way from the day Dockerfile.portal was written (C010): the compose stack
# cannot build the portals until Wave 2, and nothing else invoked this template, so the first run
# of it was cd's images job on 2026-08-10.

# Build output — 100+ MB of it, and it must never leak into an image layer.
# node_modules is rebuilt by `npm ci` in the deps stage; copying the host's would defeat the
# lockfile and can carry the wrong platform's binaries.
**/node_modules/
**/.next/
**/out/
**/bin/
**/obj/
**/build/
**/.gradle/

.git/
.github/

# Nothing outside portals/ contributes to a portal image.
apps/
backend/
db/
docs/
infra/
specs/
shared/
gradle/
gradlew
gradlew.bat
*.gradle.kts
gradle.properties
global.json

# Never copy a real environment file into an image (infra/CLAUDE.md). A portal bundle is served to
# a browser, so this matters more here than anywhere else: `.env.local` in a Next.js build is
# inlined into client JavaScript for every NEXT_PUBLIC_ key and shipped to every visitor.
.env
.env.*
!*.env.example
!.env.*.example

**/*.user
**/.vs/
**/.vscode/
**/.idea/
**/*.log
